rca_of_issue:
BUG: Stripe session_id leaks into Google OAuth flow.
Reproduction: 1) Register a user. 2) Visit /billing/success?session_id=cs_test_anything (any string). 3) Observe console: 'Failed to load resource: 401' from /api/auth/google + unhandled rejection 'Google sign-in failed'.
Root cause: /app/frontend/src/auth.tsx lines 88-110 — the AuthProvider useEffect runs on every app mount and parses any `session_id=...` from window.location.search/hash via extractSessionId(). It assumes that param exclusively belongs to Google OAuth callback (returning from auth.emergentagent.com) and POSTs to /api/auth/google. But Stripe also uses `?session_id={CHECKOUT_SESSION_ID}` on its success_url, so this collision triggers a spurious Google sign-in attempt against the Stripe checkout session id.
Mitigation: Guard the Google session capture by path. Suggested patch:
```tsx
if (Platform.OS === 'web' && typeof window !== 'undefined') {
  const path = window.location.pathname || '';
  const isStripeReturn = path.startsWith('/billing/');
  const sid = !isStripeReturn ? (extractSessionId(window.location.hash) || extractSessionId(window.location.search)) : null;
  if (sid) { ... }
}
```
Or wrap processSessionId in try/catch so a 401 from a non-Google session id is silently ignored. Either way the noisy uncaught error on the Stripe return page will disappear.
